# Exercise 06: Scenario 05 — PowerShell Administration and Security

**Author:** kuronoi  
**Project:** Windows System Administration Lab

## 1. Introduction
This exercise focuses on PowerShell administration, command discovery and security practices when working with scripts.

**Note:** This was a simulated training exercise. No actual security settings or execution policies were changed.

## 2. Objectives
- Identify available PowerShell commands.
- Understand how to identify the current Windows user.
- Understand PowerShell execution policies.
- Recognise risks associated with unknown scripts.
- Apply basic security practices during system administration.

## 3. Commands and Concepts Covered

| Command / Concept | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Get-Command` | Finds available PowerShell commands. |
| `whoami` | Displays the current user identity. |
| Execution Policies | Control conditions under which PowerShell scripts run. |
| Least Privilege | Ensures users and processes receive only necessary permissions. |

## 4. Key Lessons
- PowerShell supports system administration and automation.
- Unknown scripts should be inspected before execution.
- Execution policies provide safeguards but are not a complete security boundary.
- Administrative permissions should only be used when necessary.
- Verifying commands before execution helps prevent accidental system changes.

## 5. Conclusion
The exercise improved my understanding of PowerShell administration, command discovery and basic security practices.

